#Donor/acceptor classification for the dynamic hydrogen-bond mechanism
#(distance-only Morse potential between donor and acceptor heavy atoms, no
#explicit H needed -- see the hbond.* settings in doc/MSP_Options.md).
#
#Same classification as used by standard structural-biology H-bond tools
#(HBPLUS/McDonald & Thornton, Reduce/Probe): not invented here, just encoded
#for BioSpring's own per-residue lookup.
#
#Format: <resname> <atomname> <donor> <acceptor>, donor/acceptor are 0 or 1
#(a hydroxyl oxygen is both: it carries a donatable H and has a lone pair to
#accept one).
#
#Keyed by plain, unreduced PDB atom names -- use this table when the
#topology was built without --grp/--ff (e.g. plain -cutoff spring
#creation). For a topology reduced with --grp amber.grp --ff amber.ff,
#particles are renamed (e.g. ALA:N -> AN), so use amber.hbond instead,
#which carries the same classification keyed by those renamed types.
#
#Backbone (every residue, standard rules below):
#  N  1 0  -- amide H (absent for Pro, which has no entry for N here)
#  O  0 1
#
#His is listed donor+acceptor on both ring nitrogens: without an explicit H,
#the actual tautomer/protonation state is unknown, so either ring nitrogen
#may act as donor or acceptor depending on the real structure.
#
#Cys SG is a weak, optional donor/acceptor (sulfur, much less electronegative
#than N/O) -- included for completeness, matching HBPLUS, but expect it to
#contribute far less than a N/O-mediated hydrogen bond.
#
#SECOND ANTECEDENT, sixth column. A backbone amide nitrogen is planar with
#two heavy neighbours -- CA and the PREVIOUS residue's C -- and its hydrogen
#points opposite their bisector. Naming only CA puts the reference direction
#58 degrees off: measured on ubiquitin's alpha helix, cos^2(theta) comes out
#at 0.28 where the real N-H direction gives 0.91, so every backbone bond
#loses a factor 3.2 on geometry that is in fact ideal. With both named, the
#bisector reproduces the true direction to 0.7 degrees and recovers 99 % of
#the weight -- the geometric form of the rule DSSP has used since Kabsch &
#Sander 1983. "-C" is the previous residue's C, same +/- convention as
#.rbody and .bi.ff. A chain's first residue simply has none and falls back
#to the single-antecedent direction.

#THE BACKBONE, SAID ONCE. It is the same in every amino acid -- that is what
#makes it a backbone -- so '*' carries it, and the residues that differ say so
#below and win.

*    N     1 0 CA -C
*    O     0 2 C

#Proline's ring nitrogen is a secondary amine: no amide hydrogen, so it is NOT
#a donor. Declared with capacity 0 so it OVERRIDES the wildcard -- leaving it
#out would let '*' tag it, which is a chemistry error and the reason helices
#break at proline.
PRO  N     0 0 CA -C

#The N-methyl cap's nitrogen hangs off its own methyl, not an alpha carbon.
NME  N     1 0 CH3

#The acetyl cap needs no exception: it has no nitrogen for '*' to find, and
#its carbonyl oxygen is exactly what the wildcard says.

#THE SIDE CHAINS, which genuinely differ residue by residue.
#
#The '~' column on the exocyclic amines and the amide oxygens is NOT optional.
#An sp2 centre with one heavy neighbour and two hydrogens (or two lone pairs)
#has TWO directions at +/- 62 degrees of the plane, not one along the bond: with
#a single direction the angular weights of a Watson-Crick pair come out at 0.03
#instead of 0.93 and base pairing does not hold. This file had lost the six
#columns below while amber.hbond kept them -- the two were not interchangeable,
#and comparing only their capacity columns said they were.

ARG  NE    1 0 CD
ARG  NH1   2 0 CZ   ~NE
ARG  NH2   2 0 CZ   ~NE
ASN  ND2   2 0 CG   ~OD1
ASN  OD1   0 2 CG   ~ND2
ASP  OD1   0 2 CG
ASP  OD2   0 2 CG
CYS  SG    1 1 CB
GLN  NE2   2 0 CD   ~OE1
GLN  OE1   0 2 CD   ~NE2
GLU  OE1   0 2 CD
GLU  OE2   0 2 CD
HIS  ND1   1 1 CE1
HIS  NE2   1 1 CE1
LYS  NZ    3 0 CE
SER  OG    1 2 CB
THR  OG1   1 2 CB
TRP  NE1   1 0 CD1
TYR  OH    1 2 CZ
